| A string indexes and ranges like an array, in **characters** — the same
| rule `Length` counts by, since `strlen` reported 6 for "héllo".
s = "héllo"
assert s[0] = "h"
assert s[1] = "é"
assert s[length - 1] = "o"

| One character comes back as a one-character string: there is no character
| kind here, and there are only six.
assert s[0] ∈ String

| Out of range is null, exactly as an array's is.
assert s[9] = null
assert s[0 - 1] = null
assert s[1.5] = null

| Ranges, in the same interval notation.
assert s[0, length) = "héllo"
assert s[0, 2) = "hé"
assert s[1, length) = "éllo"
assert s[0, length - 1) = "héll"
assert s (0, length) = "éllo"
assert s[0, length - 1] = "héllo"

| Bounds clamp, and an empty range is the empty string rather than null.
assert s[0, 99) = "héllo"
assert s[0 - 5, 2) = "hé"
assert s[3, 1) = ""
assert s[9, 9) = ""

| Neither operand is disturbed — a range builds a new value, like `+`.
assert s = "héllo"

| What it is for: taking a path apart without asking a module to do it.
| `router`'s `Segment` handler exists because this was not expressible.
p = "/auth-web/account"
rest = p[1, length)
cut = 0
loop i, c over [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8]
    if cut = 0
        if rest[i] = "/", cut = i
assert rest[0, cut) = "auth-web"
